joe swinson you have changed  the liberal democrat position on brexit  from a people's vote  to revoking do you worry that  overturning the result of that 2016  referendum will to quote norman lamb  leave half the country  angry and the social contract in this  country broken  to be clear our policy is still that a  people's vote  is the best way to get out of this  brexit impasse that we should put a  specific brexit proposal to the people  to have the final say and we've argued  for that for three and a half years  and we will continue to argue for that  but in the circumstances  which can arise where we find ourselves  in a general election  that might be the last chance for the  public to have their say  on brexit as a party that strongly  believes  in our place in the european union that  that is our best  future that we should stop brexit we  have to give  the people that clear choice and say  that a liberal democrat majority  government would stop brexit  by revoking article 50. and are you  using that position because  deep down you don't think you'll ever  have to get to the point where you will  try  to revoke article 50 or would you  genuinely like to do that if you want a  general election  i would love to do that but i think  brexit is  catastrophic for our country now and for  generations to come  i think our place in the european union  is  where we have influence it's how we  secure our prosperity and trade with  countries where we don't feel we have to  compromise our values to  reach those accommodations and deals  it's how we tackle the big challenges  that we face whether that's  international terrorism or the climate  emergency we need to work with other  countries  to do that so i want to keep us in the  eu  and i want to build a liberal society  that is open  fair and inclusive so absolutely i want  to win a majority and be  prime minister there is a legitimacy  question in all of this let's just say  that the liberal democrats won a  majority at the next election let's just  put that out there as an idea in 2015  david cameron won  a general election he won 37  of the popular vote he won 11 million  votes  from the public and that gave him you  could argue a mandate  if you won you would be prepared to win  on that level of votes perhaps  but over the turn 17.4 million votes  in a referendum it's anti-democratic joe  swinson well  i think if we win a majority in  parliament and ignore the 17.4 million  if we win a majority in parliament on  the very  clear position of stopping brexit  then that will be a mandate to do that  now  i want to change our voting system beth  you know that i think that we should  have a different voting system but let's  just go back this is the system that we  have  and incidentally it's a system that does  not make it easy  for third parties to break through and  so if we are able to  achieve that which i do believe is  possible because  of the strength of feeling on these  issues in the country  if we do that then i think people will  expect us  to fulfill what we've said we would do  in that election campaign  but do you accept the 17.4 million  people  who voted to leave the eu in 2016  that you coming in and saying i'm now  going to overturn the result of that  referendum  that is anti-democratic there's no other  way of cutting it  i i i fundamentally disagree  democracy did not end on the 23rd of  june 2016. i've spoken to plenty of  people  who have changed their mind since that  time there are plenty of people  who did not have a say in that  referendum and who  are now able to vote who are in our  society  who are adults and part of our community  so  you know it's not as if that is set in  an aspect forever there are also 17.4  million people that did have their say  that voted to leave  over three years later we still haven't  left and now you are saying actually i'm  going to revoke  i'm not going to do it at all i mean and  then you say you're a liberal democrat  you're a democrat that scientific  democrats  no it's about giving people the choice  i'm standing up for my position  for my party's position of what we  believe in  what we think is the best future for the  country and then letting  the public choose and i'll happily have  a discussion about how we change our  voting system  to make it fairer that is another  passion of mine because i think we need  to reform our politics in a whole  host of ways our country needs  significant change and reform  more broadly and the first step has to  be  protecting our place in the european  union because that is how we can protect  jobs  how we can protect our public services  and how we can protect our international  influence and security but jason going  back to what norman  lam said about being people being angry  this is actually the politics of  division isn't it you are like  an iteration of nigel farage on the  other end of the spectrum  he is the brexit party and you are  turning the liberal democrats into the  non-brexit stop  brexit party you are as extreme as him  just on the other side of the debate and  i think that could turn a lot of  voters off well i fundamentally disagree  i fundamentally disagree we are clear  on our position on european union  membership  but i respect the views of people who  take a different view i recognize they  hold those in genuine good faith  and perhaps most importantly i believe  that every single person  in our country is valuable and should be  respected  and have equality and nigel farage is  prepared to stand in front of a breaking  point poster  that is designed to stoke fear and  hatred and division in our communities  and what we are proposing in terms of an  inclusive fair and liberal society is  fundamentally you're proposing to  overturn the result of a popular vote  because you didn't like how it went  that's what we are proposing to give  people the choice  ideally in a people's vote on a specific  brexit deal  which we have argued for and if that is  not possible  to give people the choice in a general  election i would like to leave and have  a referendum  on a deal with an actual vote i would  prefer  to have a referendum on a specific  brexit deal  that gives people the chance to choose  this is what brexit looks like  do you want to go ahead with that or do  you prefer so why do we have  why revoking manifesto policy because it  may be that this general election  after three and a half years of brexit  impasse  is the last chance that people have and  the logical extension of the point that  you're putting to me  is that there should be no option for  people in such a general election  to be able to choose to stop brexit and  i i disagree  i mean if a democracy cannot change its  mind it ceases to be a democracy and  it's not me that said that first it was  david davis the leading brexiteer  you you're happy that the 2014 scottish  referendum vote stands because what you  liked it  and scotland voted to remain part of the  united kingdom  but you're not happy that the 2016  brexit referendum vote stands  because it didn't go the liberal  democrats way that seems really  undemocratic to me  it's actually a very consistent position  that we think that we are better working  with other countries  we are better working with our closest  neighbors to solve our shared problems  and it is the same argument for scotland  being part of the uk  family of nations as for the uk working  within the european union  where we can better address the  challenges that we face  and what we have right now is a  situation of chaos  we have jobs being lost we have  uncertainty  we need a way out of that i think the  best way out is a people's vote  but in a general election then i will  argue that we should stop brexit  altogether  okay just couple more things jason just  in terms of a general election  it could be that the liberal democrats  are the power broker  after a general election does  re